  "name": "Ion diode",
  "description": "An ion diode turned inside out: the ring is the cathode, emitting electrons from its inner face, and the rod in its middle, on your supply, emits ions. Without a magnetic field the electrons rush to the rod and waste the supply's current. Your solenoid (the yellow coil, top right) curls them: above Hull's critical field they cannot reach the rod, and stay in a sheath along the ring. The heavy ions still cross, and the sheath's negative charge pulls more of them across. Deliver an ion current between 5.5 and 8 to the ring while at most 0.05 of electrons leak to the rod (averaged over $t = 20$ to $30$). The critical field grows with the voltage, as $B \\propto \\sqrt{V}$, and the sheath helps the ions most just above it.",
